# AddressSanitizer suppressions for LightVulkanGraphics.
#
# Usage
# -----
# CMake wires this in automatically for CTest when configured with
# -DLVG_ENABLE_SANITIZERS=ON, so `ctest` needs no extra setup.
#
# To run a sanitized binary by hand (an example, or your own application), point
# ASAN_OPTIONS at this file:
#
#     ASAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=/path/to/sanitizers.supp:detect_leaks=0 ./your_app
#
# `detect_leaks=0` is not required, but the Vulkan loader and the GPU driver both
# retain allocations for the process lifetime, so LeakSanitizer is noisy against
# anything that creates a device.
#
# Note that ASan suppressions only apply to errors raised inside an intercepted
# function (memcpy, strcpy, ...). They cannot suppress an arbitrary
# heap-buffer-overflow detected in ordinary compiled code -- so if a new error appears
# that is NOT in this list, it is real and belongs to us. Suppressing the entry below
# is what made a genuine use-after-free in Font::bake visible in the first place.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# amdvlk64.so: over-read of VkSwapchainCreateInfoKHR::pQueueFamilyIndices
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The AMD open-source Vulkan driver memcpy's 16 bytes out of the buffer backing
# pQueueFamilyIndices during vkCreateSwapchainKHR, while the application correctly
# supplies queueFamilyIndexCount = 2 (that is, 8 bytes). With the validation layers
# loaded the layer deep-copies the array into an 8-byte heap allocation, and the
# driver's 16-byte read runs off the end of it, which is what ASan reports:
#
#     READ of size 16 ... 0 bytes after 8-byte region
#       #0 memcpy
#       #1 amdvlk64.so
#       #2 libvulkan.so.1
#       #3 libVkLayer_khronos_validation.so
#       #4 lightGraphics::VkApp::createSwapChain()
#
# This is driver-side and entirely unrelated to LVGUI. It reproduces identically on a
# pristine `-DLVG_BUILD_UI=OFF` build with no GUI code compiled in at all, and it is a
# read rather than a write, so it cannot corrupt application state. It was verified as
# pre-existing by building both configurations and comparing.
#
# Scope is narrowed to the driver library so an intercepted call from any other
# library -- including our own code -- is still reported.
interceptor_via_lib:amdvlk64.so
